{"revision_id":85,"metadata":{"citation_count":285,"publication_info":[{"year":2012,"artid":"024","page_start":"024","journal_title":"JHEP","journal_record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/journals/1213103"},"journal_volume":"04"}],"citation_count_without_self_citations":253,"core":true,"dois":[{"value":"10.1007/JHEP04(2012)024"}],"titles":[{"title":"The Effective Field Theory of Multifield Inflation"},{"title":"The Effective Field Theory of Multifield Inflation","source":"arXiv"}],"$schema":"https://inspirehep.net/schemas/records/hep.json","authors":[{"uuid":"5f7f874f-70b8-48ed-88bc-83c219f45119","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1033719"},"full_name":"Senatore, Leonardo","affiliations":[{"value":"Princeton, Inst. Advanced Study","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903138"}},{"value":"Stanford U., Phys. Dept.","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903222"}},{"value":"KIPAC, Menlo Park","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/909388"}}],"signature_block":"SANATARl"},{"uuid":"c0530e88-0237-47ef-a191-2ee8ddd7337f","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/authors/1019663"},"full_name":"Zaldarriaga, Matias","affiliations":[{"value":"Princeton, Inst. Advanced Study","record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/institutions/903138"}}],"signature_block":"ZALDARAGm","curated_relation":true}],"curated":true,"figures":[{"key":"71c026e17d9c48fdd3072eed1f6853bd","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/71c026e17d9c48fdd3072eed1f6853bd","source":"arxiv","caption":"noimg: \\small Signatures in Multi-field Inflation. In the first column we give the operator generating the non-Gaussian signal: operators quartic in the $\\sigma$'s lead to a four-point function, operators cubic in the $\\sigma$'s lead to a three-point function. In the second and third columns we explain with which dispersion relation the signal can be generated. In the third we explain if the signal can appear in the Adiabatic (Ad.) or the Isocurvature (Iso.) fluctuations. In the fourth we state the potential origin of the signal. Here Ab. stands for Abelian; non-Ab. stands for non-Abelian, S stands for supersymmetry, and R stands for generated by non-linearities at reheating. The subscript $_s$ indicates that the term is generated by soft-breaking terms. The symbol $^\\dag$ represents that such a signal can be generated in the case the soft symmetry breaking term is such that it forbids some of the lowest dimensional terms. The symbol $^\\star$ represents the fact that the signal is in general subleading, but still possibly detectable. In the last column we explicitly mention if the induced signal has a non-vanishing squeezed limit and is therefore detectable also in clustering statistics of collapsed objects.: \\small Signatures in Single-Clock Inflation with a continuous shift symmetry. The signal associated to the four-point function when the dispersion relation is of the form $\\omega\\propto k^2$ is quite rich, and we refer to~\\cite{Senatore:2010jy} for a discussion about it.","filename":"fig00000.png"},{"key":"e5f173d9df1bb8545d9399bfc0652852","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/e5f173d9df1bb8545d9399bfc0652852","source":"arxiv","caption":"\\small Representation of a typical multifield potential. Modes of interest for observation cross the horizon about sixty $e$-foldings before the end of inflation. Therefore, effects coming from the evolution of the filds after horizon crossing can be treated locally in real space. Our effective theory is more general than this example, as it does not assume that the inflaton is a scalar field. This example is however interesting in helping in visualizing the different scales in the problem.","filename":"multifield_potential.png"}],"license":[{"url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","imposing":"arXiv"}],"texkeys":["Senatore:2010wk"],"citeable":true,"keywords":[{"value":"n-point function","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"field theory: scalar","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"horizon: crossing","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"diffeomorphism: invariance","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"symmetry breaking: translation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"inflation: multiple field","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"non-Gaussianity","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"effective field theory","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"Goldstone particle","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"supersymmetry","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"perturbation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"velocity: acoustic","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"fluctuation: adiabatic","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"dispersion relation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"spontaneous symmetry breaking","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"unitarity","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"Sigma/b","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"tree approximation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"spurion","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"superpotential","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"WMAP","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"We generalize the Effective Field Theory of Inflation to include additional light scalar degrees of freedom that are in their vacuum at the time the modes of interest are crossing the horizon. In order to make the scalars light in a natural way we consider the case where they are the Goldstone bosons of a global symmetry group or are partially protected by an approximate supersymmetry. We write the most general Lagrangian that couples the scalar mode associated to the breaking of time translation during inflation to the additional light scalar fields. This Lagrangian is constrained by diffeomorphism invariance and the additional symmetries that keep the new scalars light. This Lagrangian describes the fluctuations around the time of horizon crossing and it is supplemented with a general parameterization describing how the additional fluctuating fields can affect cosmological perturbations. We find that multifield inflation can reproduce the non-Gaussianities that can be generated in single field inflation but can also give rise to new kinds of non-Gaussianities. We find several new three-point function shapes. We show that in multifield inflation it is possible to naturally suppress the three-point function making the four-point function the leading source of detectable non-Gaussianities. We find that under certain circumstances, i.e. if specific shapes of non-Gaussianities are detected in the data, one could distinguish between single and multifield inflation and sometimes even among the various mechanisms that kept the additional fields light.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/759730"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"0709.0293","publication_info":{"year":2008,"artid":"014","page_start":"014","journal_title":"JHEP","journal_volume":"03"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/759732"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"0709.0295","publication_info":{"year":2008,"artid":"021","page_start":"021","journal_title":"JCAP","journal_volume":"02"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/821082"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"0905.3746","publication_info":{"year":2010,"artid":"028","page_start":"028","journal_title":"JCAP","journal_volume":"01"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/840089"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"0912.2734","publication_info":{"year":2010,"artid":"008","page_start":"008","journal_title":"JHEP","journal_volume":"12"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/851270"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"1004.1201","publication_info":{"year":2011,"artid":"003","page_start":"003","journal_title":"JCAP","journal_volume":"01"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/719001"},"reference":{"arxiv_eprint":"hep-th/0606090","publication_info":{"year":2006,"artid":"080","page_start":"080",